<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Song of Songs</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Song of Solomon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Songs, also called the Song of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>, is a book of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> consisting of love poems in the voices of a woman and her lover. Traditionally attributed to Solomon and read in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as an allegory of the love between God and Israel or Christ and the Church, it is usually dated by scholars to the Persian or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/song-of-songs.jpg" alt="Song of Songs" width="320" height="601" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustave Moreau · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gustave_Moreau_-_Song_of_Songs_(Cantique_des_Cantiques)_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
